Compile/run:
     - Compile using
            make
     - run executable with
            ./zap [zap | unzap] inputFile outputFile\n";

Purpose
    Implementation of the phaseOne functions, which help to turn text into
    Huffman trees and vice versa.

Acknowledgements
    Special thanks to the TA who helped during office hours, I apologize for
    forgetting each of y'all's names

Files:

    HuffmanCoder.cpp:
        Implementation of the HuffmanCoder class, which holds a priority queue 
        of HuffmanTreeNode pointers and a comparator. This class provides 
        functionality compress files into more efficient binary strings and 
        uncompress them back into text files using Huffman Trees.

    HuffmanCoder.h:
        Acts as the interface for the HuffmanCoder class for user inputs
        meant for use by an outside user with little to no programming 
        knowledge.

main.cpp:
        Starts instances of the HuffmanCoder class to be used to zap and unzap 
        files into encoded and decoded versions respectively. Outputs an error
        if executable is not formatted correctly.

    README:
        Acts as a way to navigate and understand the program as a whole 
        including details on architectural and data structure design.

    unit_test.h:
        Contains unit tests for various program functionalities, ensuring 
        correctness.

    Makefile:
        Automates the compilation process for the program.

    input1.txt, input2.txt, input3.txt, input4.txt, input5.txt, inputA.txt,
    all_conll_english.txt, banana.txt, banana_apple.txt, ecoli.coli, hi.txt,
    sentences.txt, & works_of_shakespeare.txt:
        Various input text files used to test implementation of Zap! against
        model for encoding into binary.

    output1.bin, output2.bin output3.bin, output4.bin output5.bin outputA.bin,
    all_english_output.bin, banana_output.bin, banana_apple_output.bin,
    ecoli_output.bin, hi_output.bin, sentences_output.bin, & wos_output.bin
        Various binary files used to text implementation of Zap! against
        model for decoding into text.

Architectural Overview:
    The Gerp class implements a case-sensitive and case-insensitive search 
    system that scans a directory of text files for user-specified queries. 
    Central to its design is a vector of FileData structures, each containing a
    filename, the original file lines, and sets of indexed words in both 
    original and lowercase forms. The Gerp class manages the full lifecycle 
    of the search operation, including file system traversal, file parsing, 
    word indexing, user query handling, and result output. It operates through 
    methods such as run(), which controls the main user interaction loop, and 
    fstIterator(), which recursively processes directories and files using an 
    FSTree structure built from the input directory. During setup, fillLines() 
    reads each file line-by-line, storing text and populating word sets for 
    efficient lookup. When a query is received, checkWords() searches all 
    stored files for matches, normalizing case based on the user’s preference,
    and writes any matching lines to the output file. errCheck() validates
    whether the search term was found and provides error messages suggesting 
    alternative search options if needed. The system is designed to be 
    resilient against malformed input, with input sanitation handled by helper
    functions such as stripNonAlphaNum() and stripAndLower(). Memory management
    is implicit through standard containers like vector and set, avoiding 
    manual allocation or deallocation. Each .cpp file corresponds to a .h file 
    defining its public interface, while external files like FSTree.h and 
    DirNode.h are used to support directory traversal without exposing internal
    structures. In terms of dependency hierarchy, main.cpp (not shown) would
    instantiate Gerp and serve as the client interface, while Gerp.h abstracts
    all program logic into a single class. This layered structure results in a
    clean, modular architecture where Gerp encapsulates all search logic, and
    user interaction is kept simple and robust.

Data Structures and Algorithms:
    The core abstract data structures used in the Gerp program include vectors,
    sets, and a tree-based file system abstraction. The FSTree and associated
    DirNode classes represent a hierarchical directory structure, traversed
    recursively using a depth-first search algorithm in the fstIterator()
    method. This traversal builds a vector of FileData objects, each
    encapsulating a filename, its original lines (as a vector<string>), and two
    sets: memberWords and lowerWords. These sets are implemented using the C++
    standard library set, which internally uses balanced binary search trees 
    (typically red-black trees) to provide O(log n) time complexity for 
    insertions and lookups. This choice ensures deduplication and fast querying
    but incurs logarithmic overhead and memory usage for pointer-based tree 
    nodes.

    The most critical and expensive algorithmic operation occurs in the 
    checkWords() method, which scans all stored files for user-provided query
    terms. For each FileData, a word match is first attempted using the 
    set::count() operation, which is O(log n) for both case-sensitive and
    case-insensitive modes. If a file is found to contain the query term, it is
    scanned line-by-line and word-by-word using istringstream, normalizing each
    word with helper functions like stripNonAlphaNum() and stripAndLower(). The
    word-by-word comparison in this nested loop results in a worst-case time
    complexity of O(f x l x w), where f is the number of files, l the average 
    number of lines per file, and w the average number of words per line. Since
    this process is repeated for each query, the overall cost scales linearly
    with the total number of words in the dataset, making it the primary
    performance bottleneck.

    The preprocessing stage in fillLines() is also algorithmically significant.
    As it reads each file line-by-line, it inserts words into both the
    memberWords and lowerWords sets, resulting in O(w log n) per file where w
    is the number of unique words and n is the size of the set. Storing full
    file contents in memory using vectors offers fast random access and
    preserves line ordering for output, but at the cost of increased space
    complexity—O(f x l)—as all files are loaded into memory at once.

    While the program does not use complex data structures like tries or 
    hash maps, it favors simplicity and modularity using STL containers that
    offer a good trade-off between performance and maintainability. Future
    optimizations might include using unordered_set for average-case constant
    time word lookup or introducing inverted indexes for sublinear search
    performance. However, in its current form, Gerp provides predictable 
    behavior, clean separation of logic, and decent performance for 
    medium-scale datasets. The recursive directory traversal, set-based word
    indexing, and linear word scanning together form a straightforward but 
    effective pipeline for textual search operations.

Testing
    The first phase testing for this project including using unit tests 
    implemented in unit_test.h. This testing covered the efficacy and 
    constistency of the traverseDirectory() and stripNonAlphaNum() functions.
    All tests were executed, and assertions were used to validate expected 
    outcomes. These tests ensure the correctness and accuracy of these 
    processes could be trusted for latter portion of this project. 
    
    Additionally, six custom inputs files were written to test encoding of: a 
    single character (inputA.txt), paragraphs (input1.txt), a single word
    (input2.txt), texts with uniform frequencies (input3.txt), words all split
    by new lines (input4.txt), and empty inputs (input5.txt). All of these, in
    addition to the given files all_conll_english.txt, ecoli.coli, banana.txt,
    banana_apple.txt, hi.txt, sentences.txt, and works_of_shakespeare.txt were 
    run using the pattern ./zap zap "inputname".txt "inputname"_output.bin as
    well as ./the_zap zap "inputname".txt "inputname"_correct.bin. Those output
    files were then run through the program implementations again, once
    following the pattern ./zap unzap "inputname"_correct.bin "inputname"2.txt,
    then using the pattern ./zap unzap "inputname"_output.bin "inputname"3.txt,
    and lastly using ./the_zap "inputname"_output.bin "inputname"_correct2.txt.
    The outputs of these were then all compared to each other and the original
    input file using diff to ensure that my implementation could encode and 
    decode its own files, could encode files that the given implementation
    could decode and could decode files that the given implementation had
    encoded, all without changing any of the content within the text file.

Part that you found most difficult:
    The data abstraction that was needed to find efficient ways to store all of
    the lines in a file and all of the words in a file separately, but 
    relatedly was rather difficult.

